1911 में ऑस्ट्रेलियाई खोजी डगलस मॉसन ने कॉमनवेल्थ खाड़ी पर केप डेनिसन में अपने [[expedition|अभियान]] का अड्डा बनाया। उन्हें पता नहीं था कि उन्होंने धरती की सबसे ज़्यादा हवा वाली जगह चुन ली है। उनकी टीम को हाथों और घुटनों के बल रेंगकर बाहर जाना पड़ता था, और उड़ जाने से बचने के लिए वे जूतों पर बर्फ़ की ख़ास कीलें लगाते थे।

बाद में मॉसन ने अपनी किताब का नाम *द होम ऑफ़ द ब्लिज़र्ड* रखा। उनकी लकड़ी की झोपड़ियाँ एक सदी से भी ज़्यादा बाद आज भी वहाँ खड़ी हैं, बर्फ़ से भरी हुई, और एक ऑस्ट्रेलियाई धरोहर संस्था उनकी देखभाल करती है। उनकी कहानी [[@explorers-poles|ध्रुवीय खोजियों]] की बड़ी दास्तान का हिस्सा है।

{{story:explorers-poles}}

जब धरती नई थी और कुछ-कुछ पिघली हुई थी, तब भारी धातुएँ बीच की ओर धँस गईं। धरती का ज़्यादातर सोना शायद लोहे के साथ उसके [[core|कोर]] में चला गया, जहाँ तक कोई नहीं पहुँच सकता। कई भूवैज्ञानिक मानते हैं कि जो सोना हम आज खोदते हैं, वह थोड़ा बाद में आया। उसे वे [[@meteorites|उल्कापिंड]] लाए जो कोर बनने के बाद धरती से टकराए।

सतह के पास दरारों में बहता गर्म पानी ज़रा-सा सोना घोल लेता है और उसे धारियों में छोड़ जाता है, अक्सर सफ़ेद क्वार्ट्ज़ पत्थर के अंदर। जब ये चट्टानें घिसती हैं, तो नदियाँ सोने के भारी कणों को बहा ले जाती हैं और बजरी में छोड़ देती हैं। ज़्यादातर सोने की दौड़ें यहीं से शुरू हुईं।

{{story:supernova}}

Birds that nest on open ground have the biggest reason to [[@camouflage|hide their eggs]]. In India, the red-wattled lapwing, the noisy bird that seems to shout "did-he-do-it", lays its eggs in a shallow scrape on bare earth or gravel. The eggs are olive-brown with dark blotches, almost impossible to spot until you nearly step on them. This kind of disguise is called [[camouflage|camouflage]], and it works for eggs just as stripes work for a [[@tiger-stripes|tiger]].

Birds that nest in dark holes, like [[@kingfishers|kingfishers]], owls and [[@woodpeckers|woodpeckers]], often lay plain white eggs. Nothing can see them in there, so camouflage doesn't matter.

{{story:dino-eggs}}

In the wild, parrots live in noisy flocks. Each flock has its own [[@animal-communication|calls]], a bit like a local accent. A young parrot learns its flock's calls by copying the birds around it. Fitting in helps it find food, friends and safety.

In some [[species|species]], parents even give each chick its own call, a sort of name, which the chick keeps. A pet parrot has no flock, so it treats its human family as one, and copies us instead.

{{story:learning-languages}}

A sperm whale's head holds a huge organ filled with oil, which focuses its clicks into a beam. The clicks bounce off squid and come back as echoes, a bit like [[@echolocation|bats]] use sound to hunt insects.

Blue whales are close behind. Their deep moans can travel hundreds of kilometres through the ocean. Some are so low that human ears can barely hear them. The ocean carries sound much better than air, which is why [[@whale-songs|whales]] can talk across such distances.

{{story:power-pistol-shrimp}}

In 1608, spectacle makers in the Netherlands applied for a patent on a device for seeing faraway things as if they were close. When news reached the Italian scientist [[@galileo|Galileo Galilei]] in 1609, he quickly worked out the idea and began grinding his own lenses.

His first telescope magnified about three times. Within months he had one that magnified about 20 times. In January 1610, he pointed it at Jupiter and saw four small points of light beside it. Night after night they moved. He realised they were moons orbiting Jupiter, proof that not everything circled the Earth. Today they are called the Galilean moons, and [[@moons-of-jupiter|Jupiter's four big moons]] are still among the easiest sights for a small telescope.

{{story:galileo}}

Around lunchtime or early afternoon, editors meet to plan the next day's paper. Each section editor pitches the stories their team is working on. The group argues about which story deserves the front page and how much space each one gets.

This is where judgment counts. Is a cricket win bigger news than a new metro line? Does a story affect many readers, or only a few? A story that seemed huge at noon can shrink by evening if something bigger happens. Newsrooms always keep space they can change at the last minute.

{{story:printing-press}}

In early 2009, the BBC's [[@arabic-script|Urdu]] service wanted a schoolgirl to describe life under these rules. Malala, then 11, wrote short entries under the name Gul Makai. She wrote about fear, about hiding her uniform, and about missing her friends.

A teenage diary written in danger has power. Decades earlier, [[@anne-frank|Anne Frank]] had kept one while hiding during the Second World War.

{{story:anne-frank}}

K. Asif spent more than ten years making Mughal-e-Azam, a love story set at Akbar's court. It came out in 1960. For one song, a Sheesh Mahal, or palace of mirrors, was built on a studio floor, and filming its reflections was a huge technical challenge.

The film was mostly black and white, with a few scenes in colour. In 2004, it was fully coloured using computers and released in cinemas again. A new generation went to see it.

{{story:dadasaheb-phalke}}

Cohl's *Fantasmagorie* used about 700 drawings, each filmed one at a time. A clown changes into a bottle, then an elephant, then a house, as the lines melt from one shape into the next. It looks like chalk on a blackboard because Cohl filmed black lines and printed them reversed.

Then, in 1914, the American cartoonist Winsor McCay toured with *Gertie the Dinosaur*. McCay stood on stage beside the screen and gave Gertie orders. She seemed to obey, lifting her leg and swallowing a tree. At the end, McCay walked behind the screen and appeared on film, riding away on her back.

{{story:eyes-see}}

In [[@ahmedabad|Ahmedabad]], Uttarayan is a two-day battle. Families climb onto their roofs at dawn with stacks of kites and spools of string. The aim is to cut a rival's string with your own. When a kite falls, the winners shout "Kai po che!", [[@marathi-gujarati|Gujarati]] for "it's cut!". Children then race through the lanes to catch the loose kite drifting down.

Since 1989, Gujarat has also held an International Kite Festival. Flyers arrive from dozens of countries with giant dragon kites, kites shaped like fish and long trains of kites on a single line. [[@jaipur|Jaipur]], Hyderabad and many towns in Rajasthan and Telangana have their own kite days too, and in Delhi people fly kites on Independence Day instead.

{{story:wind-blows}}

A podcast maker records an episode and saves it as an audio file on a website or hosting service. They also keep a feed, a simple list that says: here are my episodes, here are their titles and here is where each file lives.

Podcast apps check these feeds regularly. When a new episode appears, your app notices and can download it automatically. This is why one podcast can appear in many different apps at once.

{{story:voice}}

एक बड़ा इंसान एक मिनट में लगभग 15 से 20 बार पलक झपकाता है। सब जोड़ो, तो हर मिनट कई सेकंड तुम्हारी आँखें बंद रहती हैं। फिर भी दुनिया टिमटिमाती नहीं।

ऐसा इसलिए, क्योंकि तुम्हारा दिमाग बड़ी चतुराई से काट-छाँट करता है। झपकी से ठीक पहले और उसके दौरान वह देखने वाले हिस्सों की हलचल धीमी कर देता है, और एक पल पहले जो दिखा था, उसी से खाली जगह भर देता है। तुम्हें अँधेरा नहीं दिखता, और दुनिया स्थिर लगती है। वैज्ञानिकों ने स्कैनर से दिमाग की हलचल में आई यह गिरावट नापी है। इससे पता चलता है कि [[@eyes-see|तुम्हारी आँखें और दिमाग]] एक टीम की तरह काम करते हैं।

{{story:cameras-how}}

कुछ घूँट से प्यास लगभग फ़ौरन उतरने लगती है। जबकि पानी को आँतों से खून में पहुँचने में 10 मिनट या उससे ज़्यादा लगते हैं। यानी जब प्यास ग़ायब होती है, तब खून अभी भी नमकीन ही होता है।

अमेरिका की यूनिवर्सिटी ऑफ़ कैलिफ़ोर्निया, सैन फ़्रांसिस्को में ज़ैकरी नाइट की टीम समेत कई वैज्ञानिकों ने पाया कि मुँह और गले के सेंसर इस पर नज़र रखते हैं कि तुम कितना निगल रहे हो। वे ऐसे संकेत भेजते हैं जो दिमाग़ में प्यास वाले [[neuron|न्यूरॉनों]] को जल्दी शांत कर देते हैं, मानो दिमाग़ भाँप रहा हो कि पानी रास्ते में है। इससे तुम ज़रूरत से बहुत ज़्यादा पानी पीने से बच जाते हो।

{{story:camels}}

A [[@helicopters|helicopter's]] blades are wings that go round in circles. As they spin, they push air downwards, and the air pushes the helicopter up. In thin air there is less to push against, so the blades must be bigger and spin faster.

On Earth, a helicopter's blades might spin a few hundred times a minute. Ingenuity's spun about five times faster. Engineers at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California tested it in a big chamber with most of the air pumped out and the rest replaced with [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]], to copy Mars. The low [[gravity|gravity]] on Mars, about a third of Earth's, helped a little.

{{story:wright-brothers}}

वह रास्ता ढूँढने में एक साल से ज़्यादा लगा। बेड़ा ठंड और तूफ़ानों से जूझता हुआ दक्षिण अमेरिका के किनारे-किनारे नीचे गया। एक जहाज़ डूब गया, और दूसरे जहाज़ के लोग मुड़कर स्पेन लौट गए। रास्ते में मैगलन ने एक बग़ावत को भी कुचल दिया।

1520 के आख़िर में वे महाद्वीप के सिरे के पास एक तंग, घुमावदार समुद्री रास्ते में घुसे, जिसे आज मैगलन जलडमरूमध्य कहते हैं। उसे पार करने में 38 दिन लगे। रास्ते में उन्होंने पेंगुइन देखे, और आज दक्षिण अमेरिकी [[@penguins-cold|पेंगुइन]] की एक [[species|प्रजाति]] का नाम मैगलन के नाम पर है।

{{story:time-zones}}

First the river had to be moved out of the way. Workers blasted four huge tunnels through the canyon walls, each about 15 metres wide, and diverted the Colorado through them. Then they built temporary walls to keep the dry riverbed clear.

Men called high scalers hung from ropes on the cliff faces, drilling and blasting away loose rock. It was dangerous work in temperatures that often passed 45 °C. The official count of deaths during construction is 96.

{{story:cement-concrete}}

Long before Europeans arrived, the river joined up Native peoples across the middle of the continent. Around 1050 to 1350 CE, near today's St Louis, a city called Cahokia grew up with a giant earthen mound at its centre. Thousands of people lived there, and it traded along the river with places hundreds of kilometres away. It is one of the largest ancient sites of the [[@native-nations|Native nations]] north of Mexico.

Today, barges pushed by tugboats carry grain, coal and steel along the river. One barge can hold as much grain as dozens of lorries, so moving goods by water is cheap. Much of the grain the USA sells to other countries goes down the river to ports near New Orleans.

{{story:mark-twain}}

The tower is wrapped in bands of carved writing. They are verses from the Quran and [[inscription|inscriptions]] in Arabic and Persian, telling who built what and when. Workers carved the letters in deep relief, with flowers and vines curling around them. It is [[@calligraphy|calligraphy]] on a giant scale, and it had to be readable from far below.

Many of the carvers were probably local Indian craftsmen, and some of the decoration shows lotus and bell patterns found in older temples. An inscription at the nearby Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque says it was built using material from 27 Hindu and Jain temples that once stood on the site. Carved temple pillars can still be seen in its courtyard.

{{story:rust}}

In the 1930s, America was hit by the [[@great-depression|Great Depression]], when banks failed and millions lost their jobs. On the southern plains, years of [[drought|drought]] and poor farming turned fields to dust. Huge [[@usa-dust-bowl|dust storms]] rolled across Oklahoma and Texas.

Hundreds of thousands of people packed their belongings onto old cars and trucks and headed west to California, hoping for work. Most of them took Route 66. The writer John Steinbeck told their story in his 1939 novel *The Grapes of Wrath*, where he called it "the mother road, the road of flight". The name has stuck ever since.

{{story:great-depression}}

Indian writers have done well. Salman Rushdie won in 1981 for *Midnight's Children*, a story about children born at the stroke of India's independence. It was later voted the best of all Booker winners, in 1993 and again in 2008. Arundhati Roy won in 1997 for *The God of Small Things*, set in [[@kerala|Kerala]]. Kiran Desai won in 2006, and Aravind Adiga in 2008 with *The White Tiger*.

Kiran's mother, Anita Desai, was shortlisted three times without winning. Many readers think she is one of the best [[@indian-english-writers|Indian writers in English]] never to take the prize.

{{story:translation}}

During the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], the British government moved many children out of London to protect them from bombing. Some evacuees came to stay at The Kilns, Lewis's house near Oxford.

In *The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe*, published in 1950, the four Pevensie children, Peter, Susan, Edmund and Lucy, are sent away from London for the same reason. Exploring the big old house, Lucy steps into a wardrobe full of fur coats and comes out in a snowy forest lit by a lamp-post.

{{story:world-war-two}}

Your brain stores many meanings for many words. "Bark" can be a dog's sound or a tree's skin. "Light" can mean not heavy or not dark. Normally you pick the right meaning from the context so fast you don't notice.

A pun forces you to hold two meanings at once. Researchers who study humour call this incongruity: something doesn't fit, until suddenly it does. Children usually start enjoying puns in the early school years, around the time they understand that words can have more than one meaning. Before that, puns just sound like mistakes.

{{story:brain-works}}
